mode: "100644"
text: "# Examples — filled profiles across six business types\n\nThese are abbreviated, realistic profiles to ground the agent. They show the *level of\nspecificity* to aim for. They are illustrative, not real companies. Notice that every one has\na concrete lexicon, a real point of view, and specific proof — never adjectives alone.\n\n---\n\n## 1. B2B SaaS — payroll tool\n\n- **Identity:** Payroll software for small businesses that runs payroll in under five minutes.\n- **What's different:** The only payroll tool built for owners who do it themselves, not for\n  HR departments.\n- **Point of view:** Payroll shouldn't require a specialist. Most tools are bloated because\n  they're sold to enterprises; small businesses pay for complexity they'll never use.\n- **Audience:** Owner-operators of 2–30-person businesses (buyer + follower are the same\n  person). Cares about: not screwing up taxes, getting time back. Believes payroll is scary.\n- **Voice:** Formality 2/5 · Authority 4/5 · Humor 3/5. *Confident, never preachy. Plain,\n  never dumbed-down.* Bans: \"solution,\" \"seamless,\" \"empower.\" Uses: \"run payroll,\" \"owners,\"\n  \"the IRS won't email you — we will.\"\n- **Proof:** 40,000 businesses; \"set up in 11 minutes on average\"; SOC 2.\n- **Guardrails:** No tax *advice* (point to a CPA); never promise specific tax outcomes.\n\n## 2. Ecommerce / DTC — sustainable sneakers\n\n- **Identity:** Sneakers made from recycled ocean plastic, shipped carbon-neutral.\n- **What's different:** Fashion-first sustainability — they look good *first*, the eco story\n  is the bonus, not the apology.\n- **Point of view:** Sustainable products fail when they ask you to sacrifice. Ours don't.\n- **Audience:** Design-conscious 25–40s who'd buy them even without the eco angle. Job:\n  look good + feel good about it.\n- **Voice:** Warmth 4/5 · Energy 4/5 · Polish 4/5. *Playful, never preachy; confident, never\n  smug.* Emoji: sparingly, on-brand 🌊👟. Uses: \"drop,\" \"colorway,\" \"every pair = 12 bottles.\"\n- **Proof:** 2M bottles recycled; 4.8★ from 30k reviews; featured in {press}.\n- **Guardrails:** No overstated eco claims (greenwashing risk); cite the real numbers only.\n\n## 3. Local / SMB — neighborhood coffee shop\n\n- **Identity:** A third-wave café and roastery on Oak Street, owner-run since 2019.\n- **What's different:** They roast in-house and know regulars by name.\n- **Point of view:** Coffee is a daily ritual, not a status symbol. Good coffee shouldn't be\n  intimidating.\n- **Audience:** People within ~3 miles; remote workers, students, weekend families.\n- **Voice:** Warmth 5/5 · Formality 1/5 · Polish 2/5. *Warm and human; the owner's real\n  voice.* Lowercase, friendly, lots of behind-the-counter moments. Uses first names.\n- **Proof:** community-led; regulars; local press; the roast itself.\n- **Guardrails:** keep it personal and local; avoid corporate-café tone entirely.\n\n## 4. Professional services — boutique financial advisory\n\n- **Identity:** A fee-only advisory firm helping tech employees manage equity comp.\n- **What's different:** Specialists in RSUs/options, not generalist wealth managers.\n- **Point of view:** Most advice is generic because most advisors are generalists; equity comp\n  needs a specialist.\n- **Audience:** Mid-career tech employees with significant equity; smart, skeptical,\n  time-poor.\n- **Voice:** Authority 5/5 · Warmth 3/5 · Humor 1/5. *Authoritative, never stiff;\n  trustworthy, never salesy.* Bans hype and FOMO language.\n- **Proof:** credentials (CFP), years, anonymized client outcomes.\n- **Guardrails (critical):** No specific investment advice in posts; required disclaimers; no\n  performance promises; compliance review before publishing. Record all of this so every\n  downstream skill respects it.\n\n## 5. Personal brand / creator — indie maker\n\n- **Identity:** A solo founder building in public, sharing what works and what doesn't.\n- **What's different:** Radical transparency — real revenue, real failures.\n- **Point of view:** Most \"founder content\" is performative; honesty is the moat.\n- **Audience:** Aspiring and early indie founders; \"their people\" are builders, not hustle-\n  culture fans.\n- **Voice:** the *person's* actual voice — dry, self-deprecating, specific. First person\n  always. Boundaries: shares business numbers, not family.\n- **Proof:** the journey itself; real MRR screenshots; shipped projects.\n- **Guardrails:** stay honest; don't fake metrics; keep family/private life off-limits.\n\n## 6. Nonprofit — youth literacy\n\n- **Identity:** A nonprofit getting books into the hands of kids in under-resourced schools.\n- **What's different:** Books chosen *by* the kids, not for them.\n- **Point of view:** Kids read more when they choose; \"what's good for them\" misses the point.\n- **Audiences (two):** (a) supporters/donors — want to see impact; (b) educators/partners.\n- **Voice:** Warmth 5/5 · Authority 3/5. *Hopeful, never saccharine; dignified, never pity-\n  driven.* Lead with kids' agency and concrete impact, not sad statistics.\n- **Proof:** books distributed, schools served, reading-gain data, kid quotes.\n- **Guardrails:** dignity of the kids; consent for any photos/names; impact over pity.\n\n---\n\n**The pattern across all six:** a one-sentence identity, a real difference, a defensible\npoint of view, two-or-fewer sharp audiences, voice captured as dimensions + lexicon + a\n\"sound ___ never ___\" line, concrete proof, and guardrails that would actually stop a bad\npost. Aim for this level every time, whatever the business.\n"
